{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792332013,"nonce":"itAOvdxCvuKayM/Fu6qAnfrI4vM=","title":"A page's render stops while the page stays alive; a refresh fixes it","message":"**What was seen**\nBanner served by tests/wasm/run.sh in Chrome on macOS, DevTools open, after orbiting, panning and zooming the car: the canvas stopped updating while the page itself still responded. Reloading fixed it. The console showed no error.\n\n**What is known**\nThe frame loop in a page was timer-paced: WindowedApp waited at most 1/120 s while animating and drew whenever it was dirty, so frames were submitted with no relation to the browser's animation frames and with no limit on how many the GPU had queued. Only Wayland used the redraw pacing (requestRedraw, then wait for RedrawRequested).\n\nThe page had no handler for a lost GPU device, so a device the browser took back (GPU process crash or hang) left the canvas as it was, silently.\n\n**Measured in headless Chrome, DPR 2, scripted drags, wheel and resizes** (GPU submits counted in the GPU worker)\n1280x800, timer-paced: idle 46 fps, drag 55 fps, wheel 45 fps; resizes 45 fps; no stall in 83 s.\n1280x800, rAF-paced: idle 59, drag 63, wheel 59, resize 57; no stall.\nHidden tab: 0 frames while hidden; 58 fps again within a second of showing it, three rounds.\n1728x1000 (3456x2000 pixels), rAF-paced: GPU latency from submit to done 17 ms idle, 28-40 ms while dragging; up to 4 frames in flight under either pacing. Orbiting raises the GPU cost of a frame (the glass panels' backdrop passes over a changing scene); it is bounded here, but nothing limits the queue.\nGaps of 110-190 ms every few seconds under both pacings: Ash's full collections (859f6d5).\n\n**Not reproduced**\nNo stall in about 4 minutes of drags, wheel, resizes and tab switches. Not known: what the user's Chrome did.\n\n**Done**\nFrames in a page follow requestAnimationFrame (WEB_CANVAS joins WAYLAND in paceOnRedraw). A lost device is logged on the console and shown on the page with a Reload button (hlwgpu gpu-worker.mjs, gpu.mjs; ashui-host.mjs passes `report`). Checked by crashing the GPU process from CDP.\n\n**Next**\nIf it happens again: the console now says if the device was lost. If it did not, look at in-flight GPU work: hold the next frame until the one before the last has completed (`queue.onSubmittedWorkDone`), which bounds the queue; the GPU worker would tell the xwindow agent, which answers redraw requests.","files":null}]}